  "worked_solution": "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{3})-{y}^{3} & =-8 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{3})-{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-8) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{3}))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{3}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{3}))-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{x})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (3{y}^{2}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{12}$ and $x=1.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 3-\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=1$ into the equation of the curve.\n\nAt the point where $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1)-{y}^{3} & =-8 \\\\ 0-{y}^{3} & =-8 \\\\ {y}^{3} & =8 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=2,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 3-\\frac{1}{4}(2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
